{SECT 0 {EXCHG {PARA 258 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 258 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 21 "MAPLE NOTES for MA321" }}{PARA 258 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 56 " \+
School of Mathematics, Statistics & Applied Mathematics" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 160 "These notes are a r
eview of basic features of Maple 13 and an introduction to some advanc
ed aspects. Please proceed by entering return at each > prompt symbol.
\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 259 "" 0 "" {TEXT 256 39 "\n2. Basic Maple Mathema
tical Commands. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 140 "Maple has many useful commands that allow manipulation a
nd evaluation of mathematical expressions. Here we describe the follow
ing commands: " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{HYPERLNK 17 "solve" 1 "" "solve" }{TEXT -1 2 ", " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{HYPERLNK 17 "assign" 1 "" "assign" }{TEXT -1 2 ", " }{HYPERLNK 17 "un
assign" 1 "" "unassign" }{TEXT -1 2 ", " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 
17 "subs" 1 "" "subs" }{TEXT -1 2 ", " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 
"fsolve" 1 "" "fsolve" }{TEXT -1 1 "," }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 
"simplify,expand,combine,factor" 1 "" "algebra" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{HYPERLNK 17 "c" 1 "" "sort" }{HYPERLNK 17 "ollect,coeff,sort" 1 "" "p
olynom" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 "sum" 1 "" "sum" }{TEXT -1 2 ",
 " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {HYPERLNK 17 "series" 1 "" "series" }{TEXT -1 1 "
," }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 1 " " }{HYPERLNK 17 "prod\n" 1 "" "prod
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "solve" {TEXT 257 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 271 38 "solve - Solving Equations for Unknowns" }{TEXT -1 75 ". \+
 The simplest example is solving a single equation for a single variab
le." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 18 "> \+
solve(equation);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 28 "> solve(equation, var
iable);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 67 "Similarly a set of equations can be solved for a set of v
ariables :" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 44 "> solve(set of equations, set of variables);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 61 "The notion of sets in M
aple will be discussed fully later on." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "
" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 10 "Examples:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "solve(4*x=1,x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "solve(x^2-2,x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 
49 "Note that more than one solution may be found ! \n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 40 "SolutionSet:=solve(\{x+y=2,x-y=1\},
\{x,y\});" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 158 "Note that here the \+
input equations and variables and output solution are enclosed by \{ \+
\} brackets denoting a set. Sets are discussed in more detail later on
.\n" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 5 " x,y;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 77 "Note that x and y have not been assigned to the valu
es of the solution found." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "
" 0 "assign" {TEXT 265 6 "assign" }{TEXT -1 106 " - To assign the foun
d solution to the given variables use the assign command. In the last \+
example above :" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 20 "assign(SolutionS
et);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 4 "x;y;" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "unassign" {TEXT 266 8 "u
nassign" }{TEXT -1 93 "- We can then unassign these values as before b
y use of the ' symbol or the unassign command:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "unassign('x
','y');x;y;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT -1 153 "The solve command does not always find all solutions o
r may present the solution as the root of some other equation which ca
n then be solved numerically." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 9 "Examples:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "solve(x = x+1,x); " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 27 " No solution is possible !\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "solve(x=cos(x),x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 31 "No exact solution is possible !" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "
" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "solve(sin(x)=0); " }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 85 "\nOnly one solution is found whereas the \+
general solution is x=n*Pi for any integer n." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "solve(\{y^2
+1=x,y=x+2\},\{x,y\});" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "subs" {TEXT 267 32 "subs - Substitution of variables" }
{TEXT -1 144 ".  Rather than globally assigning variable(s) given valu
e(s) we may also substitute variable(s) by replacement value(s) into a
 given expression." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 41 "> subs(variable=replacement, expression);" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 68 "> subs(variable1=rep
lacement1,variable2=replacement2,...,expression)" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 9 "Examples:" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "exp
r:=x^3-2*x*y-y;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "subs(x=y
,expr);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "subs(x=z,y=1,exp
r);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#,(*$)%\"zG\"\"$\"\"\"F(*&\"\"#F
(F&F(!\"\"F(F+" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }{TEXT 268 26 "fsolve - Numerical Solving" }{TEXT 
-1 89 ".  We may also use floating point arithmetic to solve sets of e
quations by use of fsolve:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT -1 21 "> fsolve(expression);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 47 "> fsolve(set of expressions, set of variables);" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT -1 56 "> fsolve(set of expressions, set of variables, optio
ns);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 106 "The fsolve command is used in exactly the same way as solve bu
t returns numerical floating point results. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
-1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 168 "The optional third argument al
lows fsolve to compute particular kinds of solutions e.g. complex root
s, roots in a particular range,... See ?fsolve for more information." 
}}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 9 "Examples
:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "fsolve(x=cos(x));" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 20 "fsolve(x^2-x+1=0,x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 28 "fsolve(x^2-x+1=0,x,complex);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 
"" {TEXT -1 39 "\nDemonstrates the complex root option.\n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "fsolve(x^3-x=0,x);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "fsolve(x^3-x=0,x,x=-2..-0.5);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 74 "\nDemonstrates the root range opti
on : a root between -2 and -0.5 is found." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 
0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "algebra" {TEXT 258 69 "Simplify, expand, \+
combine, factor - Algebraic  Manipulations in Maple" }{TEXT -1 1 "." }
}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 128 "\nThere are a number of Maple commands \+
that allow manipulation of expressions into different forms. Four impo
rtant examples are :" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT -1 23 "> simplify(expression);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 21 
"> expand(expression);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 22 "> combine(expre
ssion);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 21 "> factor(expression);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 1 " " }{TEXT 259 8 "simplify" }{TEXT -1 202 " -  tries to re-
express a given expression into its simplest form. It makes use of tri
gonometric identities and properties, properties of other functions li
ke log and exp and other simplifying features." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> \+
" 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 260 6 "expand
" }{TEXT -1 58 " - multiplies out any factors and trigonometric functi
ons." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 261 7 "combine" }{TEXT -1 153 " - merges terms in expres
sions. Performs in the opposite way to expand. Various options can be \+
selected with combine - see ?combine for further details.\n" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }{TEXT 270 7 "factor " }{TEXT -1 
34 "- looks for any algebraic factors." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "
" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 10 "Examples:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "cos(x)^2+sin(x)^2;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "simplify(%);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
0 23 "z:=x^2-y^2-(x-y)*(x+y);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
0 12 "simplify(%);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "poly:
=(x-1)^10;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "expand(poly);
      " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "factor(%);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "expand(cos(a+b));" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "combine(%,trig);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 76 "\nThis demonstrates the combine command i
n conjunction with the trig option.\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "polynom" {TEXT 262 20 "collect, coeff, s
ort" }{TEXT -1 57 " - Algebraic manipulations with polynomial expressi
ons.  " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 77 
"A number of specialised commands are available for manipulating polyn
omials:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 37 "p:=6*x^4*y-2*x
*y^2+x^2-y^3+2*x^2*y^2;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "
collect(p,x);collect(p,y);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 72 "Thi
s command collects together the coefficients for the given variable\n
\n" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "coeff(p,y^2);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "coeff(p,y,3);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "
" 0 "" {TEXT -1 49 "\nThis command find the coeff for the given term.
\n" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "sort(p,x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 82 "\nThis command sorts the polynomial into decrea
sing powers of the given variable(s)" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" 
}}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 15 "Sums and Series" }{TEXT -1 70 
".  Finite and infinite sums and power series can be defined in Maple.
\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "sum" {TEXT 263 20 "sum - Computing Sums" }
{TEXT -1 70 ". The definite sum over an index ranging from low to high
 is given by:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 33 "> sum( summand, index=low..high);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 32 "The sum can be infinite i
n size." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 
10 "Examples:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "sum(i,i=
1..100);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "sum(i,i=1..n);
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "expand(%);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "factor(%);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "sum(1/j^2, j=1..infinity);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 28 "sum(a[n]*x^n,n=1..infinity);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "series" {TEXT 264 21 "Se
ries - Power Series" }{TEXT -1 130 ".  Taylor power series expansions \+
about a given point can be defined for a given expression up to a spec
ified order of truncation:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 68 "> series(expression, variable=expansion p
oint, order of truncation);" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 
0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 140 "If no order of truncation is supplied, the ord
er is given by the value of the global variable Order which is initial
ly set to 6 by default. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "
" 0 "" {TEXT -1 9 "Examples:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 0 "" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 38 "Order:=4;logSeries:=series(l
n(t),t=1);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 32 "cosSeries:=se
ries(cos(x),x=0,7);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "subs
(x=0.5,cosSeries);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 78 "\nTo evalua
te a given power series it must first be converted to a polynomial:\n
" }}{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 35 "poly:=convert(cosSeries,'polyno
m');" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT -1 93 "\nThe convert command is \+
described after the discussion on the commands op and nops later on.\n
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "subs(x=0.5, poly);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "cos(0.5);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{MARK "0 0 0" 0 }{VIEWOPTS 1 1 0 1 1 
1803 1 1 1 1 }{PAGENUMBERS 0 1 2 33 1 1 }
